<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="e7f08e9d">&lt;p&gt;
    Further development of a project plan begins with the creation of the Work Breakdown Structure (WBS). The WBS helps
    define all the work that is necessary to complete the project and serves as the foundation for creating a project plan.
    The WBS is work product oriented and hierarchical, it decomposes the overall work into smaller elements that can be
    planned and controlled. Work that is not in the WBS is outside the scope of the project.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The WBS can be transformed into a project task network and schedule using a project planning tool such as Microsoft
    Project. This WBS to Project Schedule transformation involves adding work estimates, team assignments, task
    dependencies, iteration and additional tasks to make it a complete plan of the work to be done to achieve the project
    objectives. These additional tasks may derived from the WBS of other service delivery toolkits or they may be unique to
    the specific project and client circumstances.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Also, obtain input from the project manager and project team concerning percentage complete and timeline information
    for plan.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    WBS with a branch decomposed down through the activity level
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../core.mgmt.common.base/guidances/guidelines/resources/WBS_Image.png&quot; width=&quot;350&quot; height=&quot;312&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    A WBS must be transformed into a project plan and schedule:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../core.mgmt.common.base/guidances/guidelines/resources/TransferredToPlan.png&quot; width=&quot;354&quot; height=&quot;258&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    As illustrated above, there are often relationships between activities that preclude the start or finish of other
    activities. Within a project task network, there will be a critical path, of activities. The Critical Path is the
    longest path of activities through the network that has no “float” or “slack” i.e. any delay to the activities on this
    path delay the entire project.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The critical path generally identifies the activities with the least flexibility, and therefore those that are more
    likely to affect the project schedule.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../core.mgmt.common.base/guidances/guidelines/resources/CriticalPath.png&quot; width=&quot;380&quot; height=&quot;161&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The WBS acts as the vehicle for decomposing work into smaller units and a common framework from which:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Planning can be performed
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Costs and budgets can be established
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Schedule, cost and performance metrics can be tracked.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Objectives can be linked to resources in a logical manner.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Schedules and progress reporting procedures can be established.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Schedule dependency, network construction and control planning can be initiated.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Responsibility for each element can be established.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The following can be used as inputs to assist with creating WBS:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Statement of Work (SOW)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Business case
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Project proposal
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Project charter
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Engagement letter
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Previously issued reports (finding and recommendation, improvement materials, etc)
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    For small to medium sized projects, three levels in the WBS are typically sufficient.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Level 1&lt;/strong&gt; is used for the project phases. 
        &lt;ul&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;
                Highest level of the project or systems life cycle.
            &lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Level 2&lt;/strong&gt; is used for project activities. 
        &lt;ul&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;
                Logical combination of tasks needed to build a work product.
            &lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Level 3&lt;/strong&gt; is used for project tasks. 
        &lt;ul&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;
                Unit of work at which resources expend effort.
            &lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
